<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.5: Equations of Graph Translations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02053</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.5: Equations of Graph Translations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7c09d1c0-e1ca-4ed3-82cd-1866371d49c7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph that represents \(y=x^2\). </para>
            <para id="para-00002">
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00003">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. On the same coordinate plane, sketch a graph that represents \(y=(x−6)^2+10\). </para>
            <para id="para-00004">Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00006">
              <figure id="fig-00002">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_10_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00007">A graph the same shape as \(y=x^2\) but shifted 6 units to the right and 10 units upward. Its vertex is at \((6,10)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="fb53cb18-831e-4ec9-99db-988b82b6b351" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <newline/>
            <para id="para-00008">The graph representing \(y=x^2\) is shifted 2 units to the left, 10 units down, and flipped so that it opens downward, as shown. </para>
            <para id="para-00009">Which equation defines this curve?</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00011">\(y=-(x+2)^2−10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00012">\(y=(x+2)^2−10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00013">\(y=-(x+2)^2+10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00014">\(y=(x+2)^2+10\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00015">Correct! \(y=-(x+2)^2−10\)</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The correct answer is \(y=-(x+2)^2−10\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>